          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Apart from medication, what helps with long-term insomnia?",
          "answer": "Yes. The main non-medication treatment for long-term insomnia is cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I). Sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance lists it as first-line for adults, ahead of medication (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line). Its benefits tend to last after treatment ends, while the effects of sleep medication generally do not.\n\nCBT-I is usually delivered as a course of about four to eight sessions. It includes:\n\n- **Stimulus control:** using the bed only for sleep, and getting up when you cannot sleep, so the bed stops signalling wakefulness.\n- **Sleep restriction:** limiting time in bed to roughly the time you actually sleep, then gradually extending it as sleep improves.\n- **Review of habits:** looking at caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, shift work, and other factors that affect sleep. Alcohol can help you fall asleep but tends to disrupt the second half of the night (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line).\n\nSleep habits also matter. A consistent wake time, seven days a week, is the most useful anchor, because morning light sets your body clock (lifestyle-insomnia-sleep). However, the insomnia guidance says sleep hygiene advice alone is not effective treatment for chronic insomnia (condition-insomnia).\n\n**When to talk to a doctor:** The insomnia reference lists signs that warrant further assessment beyond insomnia treatment (condition-insomnia):\n\n- Loud snoring with pauses in breathing that someone else has noticed\n- Excessive daytime sleepiness\n- Falling asleep while driving\n- Acting out dreams\n- Sleep problems alongside low mood or hopelessness\n\nHeadaches on waking most mornings, or headaches that wake you at night, are also worth raising with a clinician (lifestyle-migraine-sleep).\n\nIf you have fallen asleep while driving, or your sleep problems come with thoughts of harming yourself, please seek urgent help now from a doctor, emergency service, or crisis line.\n\nI could not finish my search on general guidance for when to seek help, so this list may not be complete. A doctor or sleep specialist can assess your situation and discuss treatment options with you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
